WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.134 --> 00:00:02.701
-Our next guests are
twin sisters

00:00:02.701 --> 00:00:05.171
and Grammy-nominated musicians,
whose 10th studio album,

00:00:05.171 --> 00:00:07.773
"Crybaby," will be available
October 21st.

00:00:07.773 --> 00:00:10.576
The first four episodes of
their show, "High School,"

00:00:10.576 --> 00:00:12.978
based on their "New York Times"
best-selling memoir,

00:00:12.978 --> 00:00:15.482
premieres on Amazon Freevee
October 14th

00:00:15.482 --> 00:00:18.051
with additional episodes
airing Fridays.

00:00:18.051 --> 00:00:19.219
Let's take a look.

00:00:23.757 --> 00:00:25.225
-Next.

00:00:26.426 --> 00:00:27.426
Name?

00:00:27.426 --> 00:00:29.795
-Sara Quin.
-Tegan Quin.

00:00:29.795 --> 00:00:31.097
-Are you guys twins?

00:00:31.097 --> 00:00:32.899
-No.
-Yes.

00:00:34.266 --> 00:00:35.535
-Hey.
-What?!

00:00:35.535 --> 00:00:37.069
-Look, I know
that you're mad at me,

00:00:37.069 --> 00:00:38.771
but we don't know anybody here.

00:00:38.771 --> 00:00:40.340
Don't you think we should at
least have each other?

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:42.475
-I don't want to share
everything with you.

00:00:42.475 --> 00:00:43.709
Just leave me alone.

00:00:43.709 --> 00:00:49.315
♪♪

00:00:49.315 --> 00:00:52.685
-Please welcome to the show
Tegan and Sara, everybody!

00:00:52.685 --> 00:00:54.888
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:10.103
Welcome. I'm so happy to
have you guys here.

00:01:10.103 --> 00:01:12.271
-Alright, let me know if
I'm blocking Sara.

00:01:12.271 --> 00:01:13.305
-Okay.
-Am I?

00:01:13.305 --> 00:01:14.340
-Is that something
you worry about?

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:15.441
That's so sweet.

00:01:15.441 --> 00:01:16.709
-Well, no, 'cause I was
going to say,

00:01:16.709 --> 00:01:17.710
if you can see her,
I'll move forward a little.

00:01:17.710 --> 00:01:19.813
-Okay. So it was the reverse.

00:01:19.813 --> 00:01:21.647
It was reverse sister cruelty.
-Yeah.

00:01:21.647 --> 00:01:23.549
-So, you guys are twins,

00:01:23.549 --> 00:01:26.819
but this is not something
that you leaned into.

00:01:26.819 --> 00:01:28.988
You kind of shunned
your twin identity.

00:01:28.988 --> 00:01:30.456
-Well, we were saving it
for this sort of --

00:01:30.456 --> 00:01:33.193
the sunset part of our career,

00:01:33.193 --> 00:01:35.227
once people stopped being
interested in the music.

00:01:35.227 --> 00:01:36.362
We were like,
"What else can we milk?"

00:01:36.362 --> 00:01:37.596
-Twins!

00:01:37.596 --> 00:01:38.731
-Yeah, we were like,
"Let's finally tell people

00:01:38.731 --> 00:01:40.366
we're twins."

00:01:40.366 --> 00:01:43.469
-And this is very --
And this is a show based on

00:01:43.469 --> 00:01:45.404
this memoir you wrote together.

00:01:45.404 --> 00:01:46.673
And let me first ask,

00:01:46.673 --> 00:01:49.909
what is the process of writing
a memoir together?

00:01:49.909 --> 00:01:51.978
Obviously, you shared
the high-school experience,

00:01:51.978 --> 00:01:53.246
but you had different
perspectives on it,

00:01:53.246 --> 00:01:54.580
I would imagine.

00:01:54.580 --> 00:01:57.550
-Yeah. I mean, we hadn't
done anything like this before.

00:01:57.550 --> 00:02:00.386
And when we sold the book,
I think it was

00:02:00.386 --> 00:02:02.721
the first part of
the process was just terror

00:02:02.721 --> 00:02:04.057
because we had to write a book.

00:02:04.057 --> 00:02:06.726
And then,
a couple months into it,

00:02:06.726 --> 00:02:07.860
one of our editors was like,

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:10.563
"You need a timeline
and some structure."

00:02:10.563 --> 00:02:12.765
And we just decided
we would just tell our story

00:02:12.765 --> 00:02:15.768
in our own voices and then just
have the chapters alternate.

00:02:15.768 --> 00:02:17.669
And then there's
a lot of embellishment

00:02:17.669 --> 00:02:18.938
on both of our parts.

00:02:18.938 --> 00:02:21.040
And so it's kind of cool when
you read the book,

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:23.209
because there's stories that
come from both our perspectives,

00:02:23.209 --> 00:02:24.377
and they're very different.

00:02:24.377 --> 00:02:26.045
So it's basically like
going to dinner with us.

00:02:26.045 --> 00:02:28.915
-And when you read -- when
you read each other's chapters,

00:02:28.915 --> 00:02:30.616
were there times that
you thought to yourself,

00:02:30.616 --> 00:02:31.851
"That didn't happen"?

00:02:31.851 --> 00:02:33.119
-Yeah, I was like,
"She's lying."

00:02:33.119 --> 00:02:34.253
But I was like,
"That makes for a better story,

00:02:34.253 --> 00:02:35.287
so we should definitely do it."

00:02:35.287 --> 00:02:37.256
-Yeah. That's very good.
You embrace that.

00:02:37.256 --> 00:02:40.092
-I actually -- I remember
being in therapy and saying --

00:02:40.092 --> 00:02:43.096
-Oh, God, here we go.
-There's...

00:02:43.096 --> 00:02:45.931
No, actually, I -- Well, I've
always known Tegan's a liar,

00:02:45.931 --> 00:02:49.835
but the part that shocked me
was actually just how

00:02:49.835 --> 00:02:51.670
truly different her story was

00:02:51.670 --> 00:02:53.305
from what I thought it
was going to be.

00:02:53.305 --> 00:02:54.440
You know, we're both gay.

00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:57.777
And we, I think,
started to really understand

00:02:57.777 --> 00:03:00.179
that part of our identity
in high school.

00:03:00.179 --> 00:03:02.314
And I was ready to kind of,
like, talk about that,

00:03:02.314 --> 00:03:04.517
like, to really explore that,
like, suffering and that,

00:03:04.517 --> 00:03:07.219
sort of fear that I had
as an adolescent.

00:03:07.219 --> 00:03:09.055
And I read Tegan's story,
and she's just, like,

00:03:09.055 --> 00:03:12.291
not in touch
with that part of herself.

00:03:12.291 --> 00:03:15.628
And so I was like --
-Well, no. I just didn't suffer.

00:03:15.628 --> 00:03:17.496
-Yeah.
-Okay.

00:03:17.496 --> 00:03:19.632
-Yeah. That's fair too, yeah.

00:03:19.632 --> 00:03:21.767
-Did you guys come out
to each other?

00:03:21.767 --> 00:03:22.902
-No.
-No.

00:03:22.902 --> 00:03:24.570
-Oh, interesting.
-I'm gay.

00:03:24.570 --> 00:03:27.940
[ Laughter ]

00:03:27.940 --> 00:03:29.441
-I think we all know.
-Yeah.

00:03:29.441 --> 00:03:30.877
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:03:30.877 --> 00:03:32.612
-We didn't --
That's yet another thing.

00:03:32.612 --> 00:03:34.246
Not to be like,
"Our book so is cool."

00:03:34.246 --> 00:03:36.315
But I do think,
you know, the twin experience

00:03:36.315 --> 00:03:38.484
has not really been explored.

00:03:38.484 --> 00:03:40.253
And I think --
Like, for other people.

00:03:40.253 --> 00:03:42.221
Like, And I spent most of
my adult life

00:03:42.221 --> 00:03:44.123
having people come up
and be like,

00:03:44.123 --> 00:03:45.625
"Can you read
each other's minds?

00:03:45.625 --> 00:03:48.227
If I pinch Sara,
can you feel it?"

00:03:48.227 --> 00:03:50.997
And, like -- But the more
common thing is just to assume

00:03:50.997 --> 00:03:53.032
that we must be best friends
and tell each other everything.

00:03:53.032 --> 00:03:55.301
But it's actually
really hard to have somebody

00:03:55.301 --> 00:03:56.502
that looks like you,
is going through

00:03:56.502 --> 00:03:57.770
all the same things
that you are.

00:03:57.770 --> 00:03:59.639
Like, there's this --
Especially as adolescents,

00:03:59.639 --> 00:04:01.307
we really wanted to be
our own people.

00:04:01.307 --> 00:04:03.275
And so I think when we were
both exploring our sexuality,

00:04:03.275 --> 00:04:04.677
it was, like, scary.

00:04:04.677 --> 00:04:07.513
I was watching Sara go through
it, and she was tormented.

00:04:07.513 --> 00:04:10.049
And I think -- I felt like
she was going to think

00:04:10.049 --> 00:04:11.250
I was copying her.

00:04:11.250 --> 00:04:12.752
And so it was like --
It was just a secret.

00:04:12.752 --> 00:04:15.221
We were, like, running around
pretending to be

00:04:15.221 --> 00:04:16.455
different people,
even to each other.

00:04:16.455 --> 00:04:17.723
-That's really amazing.
-Yeah.

00:04:17.723 --> 00:04:19.992
-I do think that that is
completely counterintuitive

00:04:19.992 --> 00:04:21.293
to what people
think about twins.

00:04:21.293 --> 00:04:22.661
-Yeah. And you see it
on the show,

00:04:22.661 --> 00:04:23.762
I mean, which is kind of cool.

00:04:23.762 --> 00:04:25.130
Like, we really lean into that.

00:04:25.130 --> 00:04:28.901
-Another thing that's cool
is you cast twins

00:04:28.901 --> 00:04:32.037
who you already were
following them on TikTok?

00:04:32.037 --> 00:04:33.139
Is that right?

00:04:33.139 --> 00:04:34.940
-No, we weren't
following them, but we --

00:04:34.940 --> 00:04:38.244
-The algorithm gave them to us.
-Yes.

00:04:38.244 --> 00:04:41.313
Like, if you -- Like --
-It was, like --

00:04:41.313 --> 00:04:43.349
-They're like, "You guys want to
check out some twins?"

00:04:43.349 --> 00:04:44.784
-Yeah.

00:04:44.784 --> 00:04:47.520
Because, like, "You've seen
two hours of clean Tok today.

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:50.456
How about some twins?"
And I was like...

00:04:50.456 --> 00:04:52.191
-That is quite literally
what happened.

00:04:52.191 --> 00:04:54.526
We were talking a lot
about twins

00:04:54.526 --> 00:04:56.429
in front of our
cell-- cellphones.

00:04:56.429 --> 00:04:58.263
I was about to say,
"Cellular phones."

00:04:58.263 --> 00:05:01.267
And we had not yet begun
the casting process,

00:05:01.267 --> 00:05:02.802
but we were talking about
twins a lot.

00:05:02.802 --> 00:05:05.805
And Tegan sent me Railey,
who actually plays Tegan

00:05:05.805 --> 00:05:07.840
on the show --
she sent me her TikTok

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:11.910
and said, "I mean, these girls
could play us on our show."

00:05:11.910 --> 00:05:14.446
And from that moment forward,
we really were, like,

00:05:14.446 --> 00:05:15.948
advocating for them
all the time.

00:05:15.948 --> 00:05:18.684
They're not actors.
They were not musicians.

00:05:18.684 --> 00:05:21.086
They did have a lot of likes on
TikTok, like millions of likes,

00:05:21.086 --> 00:05:22.488
and so they felt famous.

00:05:22.488 --> 00:05:24.690
-So the most important thing
that people look for these days.

00:05:24.690 --> 00:05:26.359
-People like them. Right. Yeah.

00:05:26.359 --> 00:05:27.926
-Yeah, we -- You know what?

00:05:27.926 --> 00:05:29.095
I think Tegan and I are --

00:05:29.095 --> 00:05:31.531
We're good musicians
and we can do what we do,

00:05:31.531 --> 00:05:33.366
but, I mean,
I think everyone agrees

00:05:33.366 --> 00:05:34.600
it's our charm
that really got us this far.

00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:36.135
-Yeah.

00:05:36.135 --> 00:05:37.136
-I mean, certainly, if they
didn't know before tonight,

00:05:37.136 --> 00:05:39.171
they know now, yeah.

00:05:39.171 --> 00:05:42.408
-But we found Railey and then we
started obsessing over them.

00:05:42.408 --> 00:05:43.842
And when it came time
to cast the show,

00:05:43.842 --> 00:05:45.778
we went through
the normal procedure of, like,

00:05:45.778 --> 00:05:47.613
looking at a lot of
really wonderful twins.

00:05:47.613 --> 00:05:49.648
But we just kept coming back to
Seazynn and Railey.

00:05:49.648 --> 00:05:53.218
And they remind us of the way
we were when we were younger.

00:05:53.218 --> 00:05:54.786
And they're from Fresno.

00:05:54.786 --> 00:05:57.823
They were working in --
-Like, a pizza place.

00:05:57.823 --> 00:05:58.824
-Yeah, they were
working in pizza.

00:05:58.824 --> 00:06:03.362
-"In pizza."
-And we were --

00:06:03.362 --> 00:06:07.066
-If you can get your foot
in the door in pizza...

00:06:08.834 --> 00:06:10.636
-And they can always go back
if they want to.

00:06:10.636 --> 00:06:12.338
I mean, we did --
-We'll see how the show does.

00:06:12.338 --> 00:06:13.639
-We'll see how the show does.

00:06:13.639 --> 00:06:16.508
But we just -- You know,
we watched them really bloom.

00:06:16.508 --> 00:06:18.076
They took acting lessons.

00:06:18.076 --> 00:06:20.980
We -- You know, they worked --
They became, you know, musical.

00:06:20.980 --> 00:06:22.948
They can sing.
They can play guitar.

00:06:22.948 --> 00:06:24.783
And they're --
They're just incredible.

00:06:24.783 --> 00:06:28.186
And we really either -- Like,
I mean, I think the jury's out.

00:06:28.186 --> 00:06:31.824
I think we've hopefully,
you know, made their lives

00:06:31.824 --> 00:06:33.492
exciting and different than
they were before,

00:06:33.492 --> 00:06:34.494
and hopefully
they don't hate us

00:06:34.494 --> 00:06:35.694
or we've ruined their lives.

00:06:35.694 --> 00:06:37.263
I don't know yet.
Like, the show's not out yet.

00:06:37.263 --> 00:06:39.665
-It could take years to know.
-We love them.

00:06:39.665 --> 00:06:41.400
-Did your --
Were your parents supportive

00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:44.270
when you decided to
pursue music?

00:06:44.270 --> 00:06:47.173
-They were definitely
very excited by

00:06:47.173 --> 00:06:48.274
the music we were creating.

00:06:48.274 --> 00:06:50.409
We'd taken piano lessons
our whole life.

00:06:50.409 --> 00:06:51.877
-But we weren't very good.
-No, we weren't.

00:06:51.877 --> 00:06:53.980
So when we started to
play guitar and sing,

00:06:53.980 --> 00:06:55.747
I think they were
more just, like,

00:06:55.747 --> 00:06:58.250
intrigued and fascinated
by what we were doing up there.

00:06:58.250 --> 00:07:00.086
You know,
we'd weave our vocals together.

00:07:00.086 --> 00:07:01.687
We were natural harmonizers.

00:07:01.687 --> 00:07:03.889
There was things, melodically,
we were doing that, I think --

00:07:03.889 --> 00:07:06.692
My parents, who were big music
people and fans of music,

00:07:06.692 --> 00:07:08.260
were like,
"Oh, this is special."

00:07:08.260 --> 00:07:10.162
And then we went through
a punk-rock phase,

00:07:10.162 --> 00:07:12.999
which kind of conjoined with
our acid phase.

00:07:12.999 --> 00:07:16.168
And I think they were like,
"Make it stop.

00:07:16.168 --> 00:07:18.537
Go to university."

00:07:18.537 --> 00:07:20.206
And then we kind of
came out of that,

00:07:20.206 --> 00:07:22.508
and, you know, a lot of really
great things happened to us.

00:07:22.508 --> 00:07:23.976
And I think that, through that
journey, they were like,

00:07:23.976 --> 00:07:25.144
"Okay, this is the real deal."

00:07:25.144 --> 00:07:27.713
-When was the first time
they saw you perform live

00:07:27.713 --> 00:07:29.114
with other people?

00:07:29.114 --> 00:07:30.516
-Like, not their friends?
-Yeah.

00:07:30.516 --> 00:07:31.951
-Because my mom would do that.

00:07:31.951 --> 00:07:33.319
She would, like, bring friends
over and bring them upstairs

00:07:33.319 --> 00:07:34.686
and be like,
"Hey, play for these people."

00:07:34.686 --> 00:07:35.921
And then she'd be like,
"Are they good, like,

00:07:35.921 --> 00:07:36.922
or are they just annoying?

00:07:36.922 --> 00:07:38.457
I can't, like -- " You know...

00:07:38.457 --> 00:07:40.893
-I think it was when we --
In grade 12,

00:07:40.893 --> 00:07:44.830
we won a city-wide
garage wars competition.

00:07:44.830 --> 00:07:46.832
And that was the first time I
remember our parents being

00:07:46.832 --> 00:07:49.335
like, "These are -- A lot of
these people in this room

00:07:49.335 --> 00:07:51.837
are strangers,
and they seemed to like you."

00:07:51.837 --> 00:07:54.840
And they were really shocked
and really excited.

00:07:54.840 --> 00:07:55.841
And that definitely --

00:07:55.841 --> 00:07:57.009
-But then they were just like,

00:07:57.009 --> 00:07:58.410
"Well, now you need to
treat it like a job.

00:07:58.410 --> 00:07:59.578
You have to pay rent."
-They wrecked it for us, yeah.

00:07:59.578 --> 00:08:00.746
-Oh, immediately.

00:08:00.746 --> 00:08:02.147
-Yeah, they turned it
into an adult thing.

00:08:02.147 --> 00:08:03.382
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:08:03.382 --> 00:08:05.818
They're like, "Congratulations
on Garage Wars.

00:08:05.818 --> 00:08:06.918
Here's your bill."

00:08:06.918 --> 00:08:08.321
-Yeah, exactly.

00:08:08.321 --> 00:08:10.556
And so, we were like,
"No problem."

00:08:10.556 --> 00:08:13.225
And 22 years later, we make a
show where we depict them,

00:08:13.225 --> 00:08:14.860
and we have control over
how they're depicted.

00:08:14.860 --> 00:08:17.229
So it's our payback.
It was a long game.

00:08:17.229 --> 00:08:18.831
-Yeah, play the long game.
-Yeah, totally.

00:08:18.831 --> 00:08:23.568
-You talk about the sort of
dabbling in some drug uses

00:08:23.568 --> 00:08:25.737
as younger people, and --

00:08:25.737 --> 00:08:27.606
-Which we're not advocating for.
-Not advocating.

00:08:27.606 --> 00:08:28.974
I think you're very honest and
clear right about it,

00:08:28.974 --> 00:08:30.175
which is very nice.

00:08:30.175 --> 00:08:34.313
But your mom --
she caught you a few times?

00:08:34.313 --> 00:08:35.982
She found some stashes?

00:08:35.982 --> 00:08:37.383
-Well, yeah.

00:08:37.383 --> 00:08:39.385
-She did -- I mean,
we thought we were brilliant

00:08:39.385 --> 00:08:41.587
and we thought we were getting
away with it most of the time.

00:08:41.587 --> 00:08:42.755
But our mom
went back to university

00:08:42.755 --> 00:08:46.192
when we were teenagers
and she worked evenings.

00:08:46.192 --> 00:08:49.060
And so we were often left alone.

00:08:49.060 --> 00:08:51.263
And one night, we had friends
over, and we bought --

00:08:51.263 --> 00:08:53.332
-Some would say "unsupervised."
-Yeah, some would say.

00:08:53.332 --> 00:08:54.666
But, like, you know, she was
doing the best she could.

00:08:54.666 --> 00:08:56.102
We love you, Mom.

00:08:56.102 --> 00:09:00.172
And she -- Yeah, she came home
in the middle of an evening

00:09:00.172 --> 00:09:01.573
where we thought
she wasn't going to be home.

00:09:01.573 --> 00:09:05.210
And she went down to her office,
where we'd been hanging out,

00:09:05.210 --> 00:09:07.613
and she managed to find, like,
a big -- I don't know how big.

00:09:07.613 --> 00:09:09.081
This big, this big --
In the book,

00:09:09.081 --> 00:09:10.583
we would say it was this big.

00:09:10.583 --> 00:09:12.952
A huge bag of weed.
And she busted us.

00:09:12.952 --> 00:09:15.854
And we claimed
it was a friend's.

00:09:15.854 --> 00:09:17.023
Chris'. We said it was Chris'.
-It was Chris.

00:09:17.023 --> 00:09:18.323
-We were like, "It's Chris."

00:09:18.323 --> 00:09:20.659
And she said, "Well,
I'll give you guys an option.

00:09:20.659 --> 00:09:23.996
We can smoke all the weed
right now together

00:09:23.996 --> 00:09:25.765
or we're going to flush it
down the toilet."

00:09:25.765 --> 00:09:26.966
And we were like,
"No, Mom, it's Chris'.

00:09:26.966 --> 00:09:28.434
We can't do that."

00:09:28.434 --> 00:09:29.935
And she was like, "Well, you'll
have to explain to it Chris.

00:09:29.935 --> 00:09:32.505
You can smoke it with me or you
can flush it down the toilet.

00:09:32.505 --> 00:09:33.873
So, in the end,
she flushed it down the toilet.

00:09:33.873 --> 00:09:37.576
But then this is
the masterful move of my mom.

00:09:37.576 --> 00:09:39.745
She stayed there all night
with us hanging out

00:09:39.745 --> 00:09:41.780
as us and our friends
all burned out.

00:09:41.780 --> 00:09:44.650
Like, we were just like --
You know, like, put a movie on

00:09:44.650 --> 00:09:47.319
and, like, had to act like we
weren't absolutely just,

00:09:47.319 --> 00:09:48.420
like, burnouts.

00:09:48.420 --> 00:09:51.290
And so we were all like,
you know...

00:09:51.290 --> 00:09:52.324
-Well-played.

00:09:52.324 --> 00:09:55.327
That's definitely a page
to take out

00:09:55.327 --> 00:09:57.129
when you're
writing your parenting book.

00:09:57.129 --> 00:10:00.733
Hey, you guys,
thank you so much,

00:10:00.733 --> 00:10:02.434
and thank you for doing
a song for us.

00:10:02.434 --> 00:10:04.136
This is really exciting,
as well.

00:10:04.136 --> 00:10:06.072
So we'll see you just after
this commercial break.

00:10:06.072 --> 00:10:08.507
You guys, that's Tegan and Sara.

00:10:08.507 --> 00:10:11.644
"High School" premieres on
